Clearing the i-th Bit :

Approach:

To clear the i-th bit (make it 0), use the AND (&) operator with the complement of the bit mask.

First, create a mask having only the i-th bit set.

1 << i

Then take its complement using ~.

~(1 << i)

Now perform AND with the given number.

If the bit is already 0, it remains 0.
If the bit is 1, it becomes 0.

Example:

n = 13

Binary:

13 = 1101

Clear the 2nd bit

Mask:

1 << 2

0001
↓

0100

Complement of Mask:

~0100

1011

Perform AND

1101
1011
----
1001

Result:

1001 = 9

Hence, after clearing the 2nd bit, the answer is 9.

Why AND with Complement?

The complement mask has 0 at the i-th bit and 1 everywhere else.

Example:

Mask            = 0100
Complement Mask = 1011

When we perform

n & ~(1 << i)
The i-th bit becomes 0 because 1 & 0 = 0.
All other bits remain unchanged because x & 1 = x.
Time Complexity: O(1)

Only one left shift, one complement, and one AND operation are performed.

Space Complexity: O(1)

No extra space is used.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    int clearBit(int n, int i) {
        return n & ~(1 << i);
    }
};

